.TH rm "" "Command"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrm [\-firtv] \fIfile ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
rm -- remove files
Usage:	rm [ options ] file ...
Options:
	-f	Force; remove unwritable files
	-i	Ask interactively before removing each file
	-r	Recursively remove all structure of directories
	-t	Test--perform all checks but do not remove any files
	-v	Verbose--report the disposition of each file
.HE
.B rm
removes each named
.IR file .
If a
.I file
is the last link to its data, the space it occupies is freed.
Removing a directory requires
.BR \-r ,
and requires write, execute, and search permission on the parent
directory.
.PP
Interrupts are caught while
.B rm
is working, so that a directory removal in progress is not left in a
half-finished state; the interrupt takes effect after the file or
directory currently being handled is finished.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Force: remove a
.I file
even without write permission on it, without asking, and do not
report files that could not be removed.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Interactive: ask, for each
.IR file ,
whether to remove it; the file is removed only if the answer begins
with
.BR y " or " Y .
.B \-i
has no effect if the standard input is not a terminal.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Recursive: if
.I file
is a directory,
.B rm
first removes every entry within it (recursing into
subdirectories), then removes the directory itself, provided it is
not the caller's current directory or the root directory.
Without
.BR \-r ,
naming a directory is an error.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Test: perform all the same checks and prompts as a normal run, but do
not actually unlink anything.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Verbose: after each
.I file
is processed, report whether it was removed or not.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ln ,
.B rmdir
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B rm
returns nonzero status if any
.I file
could not be removed -- for example because it does not exist, the
caller lacks permission, a directory argument was given without
.BR \-r ,
or a named directory turned out to be the caller's current directory
or the root directory.
Unless
.B \-f
is given, each such failure is also reported on the standard error.
.SH NOTES
Without
.B \-f
or
.BR \-i ,
.B rm
still asks for confirmation, on the standard error, before removing a
file the caller lacks write permission on, reading the answer from
the standard input; the file is removed only if the answer is
.BR y .
If the standard input is not a terminal, this prompt is preceded by a
note that there is no write permission, but
.B rm
still reads an answer from it.
Otherwise
.B rm
does not ask before a mass removal: a command such as
.B "rm *"
silently and immediately removes every file in the current directory.
